  "body": "The situation describes a breakdown in coordination mechanics rather than simple unwillingness to work. Three distinct friction points appear: semantic ambiguity in agreements, dynamic constraints via shifting schedules, and psychological withdrawal by one party. Addressing the chore distribution directly may fail if the communication channel remains compromised. The avoidance behavior suggests the conversations themselves have become a source of stress, potentially outweighing the burden of the chores. A viable path forward likely requires separating the negotiation of tasks from the execution of tasks, and establishing a medium for agreement that reduces real-time pressure.",
  "commitments": "Taking this analysis up involves treating the avoidance not as defiance but as a signal of conversational overload. It commits to testing whether written or asynchronous communication reduces ambiguity compared to verbal agreements. It proposes that any solution must accommodate schedule volatility rather than fighting it. However, this approach assumes the avoiding friend is willing to re-engage under different conditions, which remains an unresolved risk. It also implies that the user must initiate a meta-conversation about how decisions are made, rather than about who cleans what, which carries the risk of being perceived as manipulative if not framed carefully."
}
